  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 1233 AM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nHeat is here to stay moving through mid week, thanks to the synoptic \npattern remaining largely dominated by high pressure. Probabilistic \ndata even suggests 10-40% chances for highs to locally surpass 105 \ndegrees Tuesday and Wednesday afternoons. Breezy surface winds of 15-\n25 mph, along with low relative humidity values will lead to \nrecurring elevated to near critical fire weather conditions on a \ndaily basis across most of the forecast area as well.\n\nDeterministic and ensemble data show the ridge gradually becoming \nsuppressed as the jet stream atop the US - Canadian border sags \nslightly south through the week. Thankfully, the resulting southerly \nreturn flow should draw and retain higher moisture content over the \nregion daily, especially later in the work week. POPs will be rather \nlimited and confined to northern portions of the Panhandles Tuesday -\nWednesday, but are slated to increase in coverage and likelihood by \nThu - Fri. Both the ECMWF and GFS are currently forecasting PWAT \nvalues above the 90th percentile of climatology by Friday, which \nshould be taken with a grain of salt one week out, but is still \npromising nonetheless. Even if rain chances wane, there is higher \nconfidence that lower temperatures in the 80s and 90s will take over \nbehind a front to end next week.\n\nHarrel",
